{
  "@context": "../../context.jsonld",
  "@type": "Capability",
  "critical": false,
  "description": "Concentration of liquid solutions by boiling off solvent (typically water) as vapor, leaving dissolved solids behind in a more concentrated liquid. Unlike distillation, evaporation removes bulk solvent rather than separating by boiling-point differences. Operates under vacuum to lower boiling point, reducing thermal degradation and improving efficiency. Multiple-effect arrangements use vapor from one effect as heating steam for the next, recovering 60-80% of latent heat (triple-effect: 0.4 kg steam per kg evaporated vs 1.1 kg single-effect). Four main configurations: forced-circulation (fouling/scaling fluids), falling-film (heat-sensitive products, 10-30 s residence time), long-tube vertical (simple), and wiped-film (viscous products). One of the most energy-intensive unit operations; replaceable by open-pan or solar evaporation at lower efficiency.",
  "early_win": false,
  "id": "chemistry.evaporator",
  "level": "capability",
  "name": "Evaporator",
  "outputs": [
